# Code owners for the memory layer.
#
# HOW TO FILL THIS IN
#
# 1. Replace @your-org/memory-owners below with a real GitHub team
#    (@org/team-slug) or one or more users (@octocat). The team must have write
#    access to this repository, or GitHub ignores the rule silently and never
#    tells you.
# 2. Commit this file on the default branch. A CODEOWNERS file on a topic
#    branch has no effect on that branch's own pull request.
# 3. Turn on branch protection for the default branch and tick "Require review
#    from Code Owners". Until you do, this file only adds reviewers as a
#    suggestion — it blocks nothing.
#
# THAT LAST STEP IS THE WHOLE POINT. CODEOWNERS on its own is a routing hint:
# it requests a review, and the pull request merges without it. It becomes a
# gate only when branch protection requires code owner review, and only for
# branches that protection covers. Ownership recorded here and not enforced
# there is a control that everyone believes in and nobody has.
#
# Syntax: last matching pattern wins, so put the broadest rule first and the
# specific override last. A leading slash anchors to the repository root; a
# trailing slash matches a directory and everything under it. Comment lines and
# blank lines are ignored.
#
# What each rule below protects, and which gate it serves, is in
# docs/agents/github-gates.md.

# The method layer: how work is typed, addressed, gated and handed over. A
# change here changes how every future feature is run.
/docs/method/            @your-org/memory-owners

# The memory map and the one-home rule the whole scaffold rests on.
/docs/memory.md          @your-org/memory-owners

# Delivery stages, tracker rules, triage labels, vendored-skill bindings.
/docs/agents/            @your-org/memory-owners

# The checker that enforces all of the above.
/.git-memory-scripts/check-memory.sh @your-org/memory-owners

# The empty forms every node file is created from.
/templates/             @your-org/memory-owners

# Uncomment to put the CI and intake machinery under the same review. Worth
# doing once someone has changed a workflow and quietly weakened a required
# check.
# /.github/              @your-org/memory-owners
